  "top_mu": "CLE vs MIL",
  "top_mu_pct": "58%",
  "lede": "The Brewers finished 103\u201359, the best record in baseball. They lead the Dodgers 3\u20130 in the NLCS and would host Games 1, 2, 6 and 7 of any World Series. Every version of the model makes them the favorite. That still leaves a 33% chance the trophy goes somewhere else.",
  "swing": "Since the LCS began, the Brewers moved from 36% to 67% and the Dodgers went from 32% to 4%.",
  "n_games_total": "28,092",
  "bt_series": "When the model's series favorite was predicted to win 59% of the time, favorites actually won 58% across 113 series. With a sample that small, the 95% interval on that win rate is 49%\u201367%: consistent with good calibration, but not proof of it.",
  "ws_clear": "Of 11 World Series since 2015, the model's favorite won 8. Counting only clear favorites (55% or more), it's 5 of 7; the rest were near coin flips.",
  "sens_head": "The Brewers lead under every setup",
  "sens_range": "59%\u201370%",
  "coin_pct": "47%",
  "market_text": "Sportsbooks agree on the favorite: the market has the Brewers at 46% after removing the bookmaker margin, against the model's 67%. The biggest disagreement is the Dodgers: market 20%, model 4%.",
  "market_why": "One natural explanation is October deployment: the Dodgers' playoff rotation (Tarik Skubal, Blake Snell, Tyler Glasnow, Yoshinobu Yamamoto) projects to a 3.51 FIP against 3.85 for the season, and an adjustment for that moves them to 6%. But I tested the closest checkable version, adjusting each past playoff game for its actual starting pitchers (387 games, 2015\u20132025), and it did not improve predictions: it beat the plain model in only 39% of bootstrap resamples. So the gap is unexplained. The rotation story is a hypothesis, not a finding.",
  "mkt_note": "1 sportsbook sources captured Oct 14, 2026, margin removed with the power method",
  "watch_head": "What the next game in each series does",
  "watch_text": "Title odds after each possible result. The largest single swing: if the Guardians win ALCS Game 4, the Rays fall from 13% to 6%.",
  "ratings_text": "Expected runs per game against a league-average opponent after shrinkage, and the implied win rate. The Guardians rank 16th, the lowest of the four teams left.",
  "next_matchup_caveat": "It ignores the specific pitching matchup in each game. The backtest found that adding starters by season FIP doesn't help, so a better pitcher metric is open work.",
  "data_through": "Oct 15, 2026",
  "calib_note": "Dots show how often the model's series favorite actually won, grouped by how confident the model was. Each bin holds only 19\u201338 series, so expect scatter around the line.",
  "sample_caveat": "The backtest covers 113 series: enough to show the probabilities aren't badly off, not enough to fine-tune them.",
  "home_rate": "53%"
 },
